            <ArticleTitle>Analysis of the experiences of nurses who return to nursing after cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1170-1175</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.12968/bjon.2017.26.21.1170</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">understanding the impact of role ambiguity (both professional and patient) can be a factor in deepening the understanding of the ongoing personal, professional and organisational requirements of nurses who are cancer survivors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">the aim of this qualitative study was to elucidate an in-depth description of nurses' experiences of surviving cancer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">an exploratory qualitative research design was used (n=8), with participants recruited between September 2014 and December 2016.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">data analysis led to the emergence of six themes: being practical about the diagnosis and treatment, empathy and compassion, a 'new normal' and the role of organisational support, medical knowledge and treatment experiences, coping in silence and denial, and being resilient.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">nurses as cancer survivors who return to work offer a richness of experience related to enhanced empathetic responses to patients in their care and have the potential to be great practice role-models for other staff.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
